const KATZ = 'Jonathan Katz (2021), Broadcast and Byzantine Agreement, §§1–3';
export default {
  collection: 'dist', chapter: 6, title: 'Byzantine agreement',
  summary: String.raw`A Byzantine process can send a different story to every neighbor. Synchronous oral-message agreement needs both enough processes and enough independent routes; EIG and phase king attain the process bound. Publicly verifiable signatures change the broadcast problem, while PBFT combines authenticated certificates with eventual timing assumptions. Proof of work changes participation and gives a probabilistic chain model with different guarantees.`,
  items: [
    { num: '6.1', kind: 'Definition', name: 'The Byzantine generals problem',
      statement: String.raw`Fix a known set of $n$ processes and a bound $f$ on those that may behave arbitrarily. Correct processes follow the protocol. **Byzantine broadcast** has a designated sender with input $v$ and requires:
(a) Every correct recipient eventually decides.
(b) All correct recipients decide the same value.
(c) If the sender is correct, their decision is $v$.
**Byzantine consensus** instead gives every process an input. Its strong unanimity validity requires decision $v$ whenever all correct processes begin with $v$. Decisions or claims made by faulty processes are outside these guarantees.
In the synchronous **oral-message model**, a received message identifies its immediate sender, correct-to-correct messages arrive within a known bound, and missing messages are detectable. A process can lie about what someone else told it: there is no transferable signature proving that report.`,
      idea: String.raw`Agreement and validity are separate obligations. Following the sender directly preserves validity when it is honest, but a dishonest sender can split honest recipients. Taking a majority of reports can repair particular examples, yet only a proved fault threshold makes it a general protocol.
The previous chapter's crash-consensus definition used proposal validity and uniform agreement. Those conditions must not be silently carried over to arbitrary faults: a traitor can claim any input or output. Here validity refers to the honest sender or to unanimity among correct proposers. Timing assumptions also matter; detecting a missing message is a synchronous assumption, not a consequence of knowing identities.`,
      caption: String.raw`Choose a faulty process, edit its direct orders and relayed reports, and compare direct obedience with one round of majority forwarding. The counters test agreement and honest-sender validity separately.`,
      scope: String.raw`This is a binary, three- or four-process broadcast experiment with default 0 and at most one fault. A successful particular run does not establish a protocol's universal correctness.`,
      deps: ['dist-5.1', 'dist-1.3'], tags: ['byzantine', 'definitions'], refs: [LSP + ', §§2–3', KATZ + ', §1'], links: { source: 'https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/byz.pdf' }, viz: V.definitionWidget },
    { num: '6.2', kind: 'Theorem', name: 'Byzantine agreement needs n > 3f',
      statement: String.raw`For $n\ge3$, deterministic binary Byzantine consensus in a synchronous complete network with oral messages, agreement, termination and strong unanimity validity is solvable against up to $f$ arbitrary faults only if $n>3f$. The same bound applies to designated-sender broadcast for $n\ge3$. The oral-message assumptions include known identities and reliable bounded-delay channels, but no pre-established transferable authentication.`,
      idea: String.raw`Suppose a three-process, one-fault consensus protocol existed. Place copies of its identities around a six-cycle in the order $A,B,C,A',B',C'$, with inputs $0,0,0,1,1,1$. Each copy runs its own identity's algorithm, communicating with its two neighbors as if they were the other two identities. This artificial ring is not asserted to solve consensus.
For any adjacent pair, a single faulty third process can simulate the other four copies and reproduce every message delivered at the pair's boundary. Induction on synchronous rounds gives exactly the same local views. Termination and agreement in the corresponding real three-process execution therefore force that pair to decide equally. These edge equalities make all six decisions equal. But the adjacent 0-input pair must decide 0 by honest unanimity, while an adjacent 1-input pair must decide 1: a contradiction. The displayed labels are these conditional logical obligations, not measured outputs of a nonexistent correct protocol.
For $3\le n\le3f$, partition the identities into three nonempty groups of at most $f$. Three real processes could simulate the groups, including all their internal messages. A faulty simulator corrupts at most $f$ original identities. Honest groups with a common input would have to agree on it, yielding the impossible three-process protocol. A broadcast protocol also gives consensus when an honest majority exists by broadcasting every input and taking the majority. More generally, the four-copy broadcaster argument gives the same oral-message lower bound directly; it does not need that conversion when $f\ge n/2$.`,
      caption: String.raw`Edit the six initial bits and inspect each neighboring pair. Equal adjacent inputs impose a validity label; agreement propagates around the ring. A contradiction appears only when the obligations force both 0 and 1.`,
      scope: String.raw`The hexagon illustrates the consensus proof. It is an indistinguishability construction, not a finite simulation proving all impossibility results. For two participants, designated-sender broadcast is a degenerate exception: an honest recipient can simply follow the sender.`,
      deps: ['dist-6.1', 'dist-1.7'], tags: ['byzantine', 'impossibility'], refs: [MIT, KATZ + ', §3; LSP §2 explicitly distinguishes its informal illustration from a rigorous proof'], links: { source: 'https://www.cs.umd.edu/~jkatz/gradcrypto2/s21/BA.pdf' }, viz: V.lowerBoundWidget },
    { num: '6.3', kind: 'Algorithm', name: 'Exponential information gathering (Lamport–Shostak–Pease)',
      statement: String.raw`If $n>3f$, exponential information gathering solves deterministic Byzantine consensus in the synchronous oral-message model in $f+1$ communication rounds. Each process records a tree indexed by sequences of distinct process IDs. A path $xj$ records what process $j$ reported about prefix $x$; first-level paths record initial proposals. Missing or malformed reports are replaced by a common default, here 0.
After the last round, retain leaf values and resolve each internal node by the strict majority of its resolved children, or 0 when there is no strict majority. The resolved root is the decision. This full-information construction uses exponentially many path entries as $f$ grows.`,
      idea: String.raw`For every path ending in a correct reporter, all correct observers receive the same raw value. Its resolved value equals that raw value as well. Prove this from the leaves upward: at a nonleaf of depth $d\le f$, there are $n-d$ possible next reporters. At most $f$ are faulty, and $n-d>2f$, so correct next reporters are a strict majority. Each correctly reports the same prefix value, and the induction hypothesis preserves it.
Every root-to-leaf path contains $f+1$ distinct IDs, so at least one is correct. The nodes ending in correct reporters thus cover every such path, and all correct observers agree on their resolved values. Moving upward from this common cover makes the root common too. Finally, if every correct proposal is $v$, the at least $n-f>n/2$ first-level nodes with correct originators resolve to $v$; hence the root is $v$.
The model stores the entire distinct-ID tree at every observer, including paths containing that observer's ID. A report to oneself is a local copy; it is not counted as a network delivery. Each other recipient receives the reporter's previous-round prefix value. This convention makes the proof's common-node property directly inspectable.`,
      caption: String.raw`Edit the input vector and faulty IDs, then step through all receive rounds and bottom-up resolution. Select an observer and a first reporter to inspect raw and resolved tree entries. Try an unsupported process-to-fault ratio to test the hypothesis.`,
      scope: String.raw`The finite model supports 3–7 processes and at most two faults. It records all path entries and actual scalar deliveries; counts include the deliberately redundant full-information representation. A chosen Byzantine policy is one adversarial execution, not an enumeration of all attacks.`,
      deps: ['dist-6.2'], tags: ['byzantine', 'full-information'], refs: [MIT + ', slides 3–11; LSP §3 for the related recursive oral-message construction'], links: { source: 'https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-852j-distributed-algorithms-fall-2009/0d1d84b9fd1e74bf8e2f8ad916cfca0d_MIT6_852JF09_lec05.pdf' }, viz: V.eigWidget },
    { num: '6.4', kind: 'Algorithm', name: 'Phase-king algorithm',
      statement: String.raw`For $n>3f$, binary Byzantine consensus can be solved with $f+1$ distinct kings and three synchronous rounds per phase. Let $v_i$ be process $i$'s preference, counting its own message locally.
(a) Broadcast $v_i$. Set $C_i^b=1$ exactly when at least $n-f$ received values equal bit $b$.
(b) Broadcast both endorsement bits. Let $D_i^b$ count distinct senders endorsing $b$. Set $v_i=1$ if $D_i^1>f$, otherwise set $v_i=0$.
(c) The king sends its current preference. Keep $v_i$ if $D_i^{v_i}\ge n-f$; otherwise adopt the king's value.
After $f+1$ phases, decide the preference. Missing or invalid messages have the fixed default 0. The number of constant-size messages is $O((f+1)n^2)$.`,
      idea: String.raw`Two invariants prove the guarantee. First, once all correct preferences equal $b$, every correct process sees at least $n-f$ copies of $b$, endorses it and receives at least $n-f$ endorsements. It therefore keeps $b$ even under a faulty king.
Second, an honest king brings agreement. If nobody retains a strongly endorsed preference, everyone adopts its one value. Otherwise consider a correct process retaining 1: its $n-f$ endorsements include at least $n-2f>f$ honest endorsements, which also reach the king, so the king's preference is 1.
If a correct process retains 0, its $n-f$ zero endorsements include an honest endorser. That endorser saw at least $n-f$ zero proposals in round one. Every other correct observer can therefore see at most $2f<n-f$ one proposals. No correct process endorses 1; the king receives at most $f$ one endorsements and chooses 0. Either retained value consequently equals the honest king's value.
At least one of the $f+1$ distinct kings is correct. Its phase establishes agreement, and the first invariant preserves it through all later phases. This is the three-round construction; a simpler two-round phase-king variant requires the stronger $n\ge4f+1$ bound.`,
      caption: String.raw`Edit proposals, faulty identities and their message policy. Step through endorsement counts and king choices, checking which correct processes keep a strongly supported value and which follow the king.`,
      scope: String.raw`The executable model permits a deliberately insufficient process count, but labels the theorem's $n>3f$ premise separately from observed agreement. Faulty endorsement pairs may be inconsistent. Their displayed preferences have no correctness guarantee.`,
      deps: ['dist-6.2'], tags: ['byzantine', 'consensus'], refs: [KATZ + ', §2, Lemmas 1–2 and Theorem 1; modern exposition, original Berman–Garay–Perry body not read'], links: { source: 'https://www.cs.umd.edu/~jkatz/gradcrypto2/s21/BA.pdf' }, credit: 'Berman, Garay & Perry', viz: V.kingWidget },
    { num: '6.5', kind: 'Theorem', name: 'Byzantine agreement needs connectivity 2f + 1',
      statement: String.raw`For a known undirected graph $G$ with $n\ge3$ processes, synchronous oral-message Byzantine consensus is solvable against any set of at most $f$ faulty vertices exactly when
$$\begin{gathered}n>3f,\\\kappa(G)\ge2f+1.\end{gathered}$$
Here $\kappa(G)$ is vertex connectivity, with $\kappa(K_n)=n-1$. Channels identify immediate neighbors, correct messages meet known deadlines, and no transferable signatures are available. The fault bound concerns vertices, not edges.`,
      idea: String.raw`For sufficiency, global Menger supplies $2f+1$ internally vertex-disjoint paths between every pair of correct endpoints. Send a value over fixed named routes, verifying the expected immediate predecessor at each hop; missing reports default to 0. Each faulty internal vertex can spoil at most one route. At least $f+1$ reports are correct, so majority reconstructs the sender's value. Known path lengths give a finite delivery deadline. These reliable virtual links let the complete-network EIG protocol run.
For necessity, the process bound still applies. A cut of at most $2f$ vertices can be split into two groups of at most $f$, separating two nonempty sides. Collapsing each side and each cut group gives the canonical four-group network whose opposite sides have no direct link. Two copies of that network can be joined so that one three-group honest view forces 0, another forces 1, and a third honest view spans the forced decisions and requires equality. The faulty cut group simulates the missing copies, so induction preserves every honest boundary view. This is the same simulation principle as the hexagon, with the cut providing the two possible lying intermediaries. The lecture's eight-copy diagram gives the canonical construction; the graph experiment here checks cuts and routes, not that whole adversarial execution.`,
      caption: String.raw`Edit the graph's edge list, choose honest terminals and mark faulty internal vertices. Inspect a minimum vertex cut and a maximum family of internally disjoint paths; corrupted paths flip their reports in the channel experiment.`,
      scope: String.raw`Maximum path packing is computed exactly for at most seven vertices. The selected terminals may have more disjoint paths than the graph's global connectivity. A successful chosen channel does not establish Byzantine consensus on a graph below the global threshold.`,
      deps: ['dist-6.2', 'graph-3.3.6'], tags: ['byzantine', 'connectivity'], refs: [MIT + ', slides 20–26; GRAPH 3.3.6 for Menger; the general cut-to-canonical reduction is an explanatory outline'], links: { source: 'https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-852j-distributed-algorithms-fall-2009/0d1d84b9fd1e74bf8e2f8ad916cfca0d_MIT6_852JF09_lec05.pdf' }, viz: V.connectivityWidget },
  ],
};
